title: OPA and Rego, hands-on
guide: howto/opa-rego.html
promise: Open Policy Agent (OPA) checks a JSON document against rules you write. Learn what its language, Rego, does, write rules that run, use them to check a real Terraform plan before it is applied, then test the rules themselves.

== Who it is for and what you need
section: How to use this guide
It is for beginner to intermediate readers who can use a terminal and read JSON, and teaches Rego from the start.

You need the opa binary. Step 5 also needs Conftest and Terraform, with your own configuration to plan.

== How the guide was checked
section: How to use this guide
Every policy, test and command, answers included, ran with OPA 1.22.2, Conftest 0.71.1 and Regal 0.43.0 on an Apple silicon Mac; step 5's plan used dummy credentials, touching no AWS account.

Not run: brew install lines, which install software machine-wide.

== How to follow the guide
section: How to use this guide
Read it in order. Paste BASH blocks into your terminal, in the folder that holds your files. Save a REGO block only when its slide names a file. No block shows output: the text says what most commands print.

== Set up your environment
section: 1. Install OPA and confirm you are on 1.x
On macOS, install OPA with Homebrew. On Linux, or if you get 0.x, take the static binary from github.com/open-policy-agent/opa/releases. The opa version command must report Version: 1.x; on 0.x every example fails to parse.
```bash
brew install opa

opa version
```

== What OPA is and where you meet it
section: What OPA is
OPA checks a JSON document, the plain-text format most cloud tools use, against rules in its language, Rego, and returns a decision. Conftest feeds it Terraform plans, Gatekeeper feeds it Kubernetes requests and Envoy, a proxy, feeds it web requests.

== The one idea: undefined is not false
section: Undefined is not false
Step 2 saves this as bucket.rego. A rule asks about input, the document being decided about. If its body cannot be satisfied, the rule is undefined, not false: with encryption missing, this one adds nothing, and the bucket passes.
```rego
package storage        # the package path becomes data.storage

deny contains msg if {
    input.encryption.enabled == false
    msg := sprintf("bucket %v is not encrypted", [input.name])
}
```

== Step 2: evaluate it from the command line
section: 2. Your first policy, evaluated from the command line
Step 2 saves bucket.json as {"name": "prod-invoices", "encryption": {"enabled": false}, "public": true}. The -d and -i flags load the policy and the input. Expect one message. Set enabled to true and rerun: it prints [], an empty set, meaning allowed.
```bash
opa eval --format pretty -d bucket.rego -i bucket.json 'data.storage.deny'
```

== Step 3: watch undefined bite you, then fix it
section: 3. Watch undefined bite you
Make bucket.json {"name": "prod-invoices", "public": true}: the command prints [], so a bucket without encryption passes. Put this rule into bucket.rego; the not keyword succeeds when its expression is false or undefined, so unencrypted and missing both give a message.
```rego
deny contains msg if {
    not input.encryption.enabled == true        # true for false AND for missing
    msg := sprintf("bucket %v is not encrypted", [input.name])
}
```

== Step 4: iterate over a collection
section: 4. Iterate over a collection
Every rule named deny adds to one set. The some keyword binds bucket to each element of input.buckets, so each match adds its own message.
```rego
deny contains msg if {
    some bucket in input.buckets          # iterate; `bucket` is bound per element
    not bucket.encryption.enabled == true
    msg := sprintf("bucket %v is not encrypted", [bucket.name])
}

# A second, independent check over the same collection.
deny contains msg if {
    some bucket in input.buckets
    bucket.public == true
    msg := sprintf("bucket %v is public", [bucket.name])
}
```

== Step 5: a Terraform plan is just input
section: 5. Gate a real Terraform plan with Conftest
Terraform builds cloud resources from configuration files and can show its planned changes first. In a configuration of your own, these save the plan as JSON. Without one, save step 5's policy anyway: steps 6 and 7 need no plan.
```bash
terraform plan -out=tfplan.binary
terraform show -json tfplan.binary > tfplan.json
```

== Step 5: a policy over the plan's changes
section: 5. Gate a real Terraform plan with Conftest
Step 5's policy/s3.rego starts like this. In the plan, resource_changes lists each change; change.after is the resource as it will exist. The rule judges aws_s3_bucket_public_access_block, an S3 bucket's settings for blocking public access. A plan with none gives no findings.
```rego
package main        # Conftest looks in `main` by default

deny contains msg if {
    some rc in input.resource_changes
    rc.type == "aws_s3_bucket_public_access_block"
```

== Step 5: skip deletions, require the setting
section: 5. Gate a real Terraform plan with Conftest
This is the rest of policy/s3.rego. The block_public_acls setting makes S3 refuse requests that would set a public ACL (access control list). As in step 3, not ... == true also counts a missing setting.
```rego
    # Only judge resources that will exist afterwards. A destroy has
    # change.after == null; reading a field off null is undefined, and
    # `not` turns undefined into a match, so without this line deleting
    # the resource would be reported as a violation.
    not "delete" in rc.change.actions

    not rc.change.after.block_public_acls == true
    msg := sprintf("%v does not block public ACLs", [rc.address])
}
```

== Step 5: Conftest turns denials into a gate
section: 5. Gate a real Terraform plan with Conftest
Conftest wraps OPA and reads plan JSON. Exit code 0 means no denials and 1 means at least one: your CI gate. A policy that fails to load also exits 1, so read the output. Expect your own resource names.
```bash
brew install conftest
conftest test --policy policy/ tfplan.json

echo $?
```

== Step 6: test the policy in both directions
section: 6. Unit-test the policy itself
Step 6's policy/s3_test.rego, in package main, ends with this test; the first expects one denial. A test_* rule is a test, passing if true; the with input as clause swaps in a fake document. Run opa test policy/ -v.
```rego
# The direction people forget. Without this, a policy that denies
# EVERYTHING passes the test above and looks perfectly healthy.
test_allows_compliant_bucket if {
    count(deny) == 0 with input as {
        "resource_changes": [{
            "address": "aws_s3_bucket_public_access_block.good",
            "type": "aws_s3_bucket_public_access_block",
            "change": {"actions": ["create"], "after": {"block_public_acls": true}},
        }]
    }
}
```

== Step 7: format, check and lint
section: 7. Format, check, and lint
The opa fmt -w command rewrites files in place; opa check --strict rejects what parses but almost always means a bug. Regal, a Rego linter, catches idiom problems: after brew install regal, regal lint policy/ reports conventions here, not bugs.
```bash
opa fmt -w policy/
opa check --strict policy/
```

== Exercise: require IMDSv2 on every instance
section: Hands-on exercises
Write a rule denying any EC2 instance in a plan whose metadata_options block does not set http_tokens to "required", which forces IMDSv2. A missing block matters most. It needs a plan; the guide hides each answer behind Show the answer.

== Answer: not ... == "required", and [0]
section: Hands-on exercises
Testing == "optional" catches only the bad value and misses an absent block, the more common case. In plan JSON a nested block such as metadata_options is a list: without [0], the not keyword flags compliant instances too.
```rego
deny contains msg if {
    some rc in input.resource_changes
    rc.type == "aws_instance"
    not "delete" in rc.change.actions
    not rc.change.after.metadata_options[0].http_tokens == "required"
    msg := sprintf("%v does not require IMDSv2", [rc.address])
}
```

== Exercise: break the rule on purpose
section: Hands-on exercises
This exercise breaks step 5's rule so it denies everything, deleting its conditions and leaving only the some rc line and the message, then reruns step 6's tests. Which fail, and what does that tell you about those tests?

== Answer: only the compliant test fails
section: Hands-on exercises
Only test_allows_compliant_bucket fails: a rule that denies everything still denies the bad input, so the deny-direction test passes. Every policy needs a test that a compliant input produces no findings, the test people skip.

== Common mistakes
section: Common mistakes
- Pre-1.0 rule bodies without the if keyword fail to parse on 1.x.
- By default Conftest skips policies outside package main; evaluating none exits 0.
- A misspelled field passes opa check --strict and Regal; only a realistic test catches it.
